{
  "state": {
    "case_id": "235",
    "case_summary": "An 80-year-old woman presented with involuntary movements of her left upper and lower limbs for more than 10 days. She had a history of untreated type 2 diabetes mellitus. On neurological examination, she had a slow response, reduced muscle strength, and positive tendon reflexes in her left limbs. Continuous, irregular involuntary movements of variable amplitude were observed in the left arm and leg. Laboratory testing revealed a blood glucose level of 8.59 mmol/L (reference range, 3.9–6.1) and a hemoglobin A1c of 7.43% (4–6), with all other laboratory values within normal limits. Noncontrast CT of the head showed slightly increased density in the bilateral striatal regions without evidence of acute infarction. Subsequent brain MRI demonstrated high-signal intensity on T1-weighted images in the bilateral basal ganglia.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n\nOptions:\nA. Nonketotic hyperglycemia hemichorea\nB. Cerebrovascular disease was considered but excluded because “no obvious signs of stroke were seen\nC. Acute infarction was considered but excluded because “Negative changes were found on DWI and ADC images\nD. Chronic liver disease was considered as a cause of T1 hyperintensity but excluded because “other laboratory data were\nE. Neurofibromatosis was considered as a cause of T1 hyperintensity but excluded because “other laboratory data were\nF. Manganese deposition was considered as a cause of T1 hyperintensity but excluded because “other laboratory data were\nG. Calcification was considered as a cause of T1 hyperintensity but excluded because “T1WI high\nH. Brain trauma was considered but excluded because “other causes include brain trauma…” and there was no history of trauma\n",
